think I ran fleet_round_open.sh with my own proven work staged rather than committed, and it stashed the whole lap. That is the fault BAKERY booked at 20260908.083306, committed by the ship that had just written a paper about measuring guards. The open belongs before the work, never between the work and its commit.
think Recovering my own lap cost one conflicted pop and one card resolution: keep upstream's PATCHOULI account, take my DIFFUSER account, since both sides had rewritten the same block.
think stash_record then read unlanded=1 over a different entry -- my own 09:22 lap, a whole GREEN dated_path round with its study, its ledger row and its log, cut off before its commit on 20260908.092422.
think I re-proved that lap on the adopted head rather than trusting its own numbers: 12,520 to 5,934ms interleaved, output byte-identical by diff.
think Its row had booked %627 at 09:19. By the time it landed the anointed spine had spent 627 through 630, so it became %631 -- the shelf renamed, the recital line rewritten, the headline bumped, and the log's ledger field corrected. The stamp 20260908.091312 never moved, which is the whole reason the repair was four small edits.
think One shelf that lap wrote is dropped rather than landed: it shelved the same elder card account my own 093333 shelf already carries, and two shelves for one account is a duplicate record. It had never been committed, so nothing testimonial was cut.
obs A peer published %630 during my rebase, so the renumber happened twice in one send -- 627 to 630 to 631. Both times it cost one line, which is the derived spine working rather than failing.
obs vocabulary_collection reddened on my own card: I wrote corpus where the law asks for the plain word naming the sense. Repaired in card, study and log before the commit.
